# Death House, the floors above ground: props whose reading changes the story (lore, the lullaby, the deeds).
# Lore discoveries each add 1 to death_house_lore once; four or more lets the party read the altar (altar.dialogue).

~ family_portrait
narrate examine:hall_family_portrait
Narrator: A handsome family in oils above the mantel: a broad, smiling man cradling a swaddled baby, a narrow woman looking at that baby the way you'd look at a stain, a girl of ten with her chin up, and a small boy holding a stuffed wolf by one ear.
if flag.death_house_heard_plea
  Narrator: The children from the road: the same faces, the same wolf. The varnish has gone brown with age, and the dust on the frame is thick enough to write in.
  if not flag.death_house_saw_through_lure
    set death_house_saw_through_lure
    set death_house_lore += 1
  endif
  if name:thistle and name:liriel_dawnsong
    interject name:thistle: That's them. The road ones. Same wolf. That paint's older than Grandda.
    interject name:liriel_dawnsong: Then they have been waiting in that road a very long time. Poor lambs.
  else
    interject tag:curious: How long ago was this painted? And how are they still that age outside?
  endif
endif
* [History DC 12] Work out when it was painted. -> portrait_history | portrait_history_fail
* Move on. -> END

~ portrait_history
Narrator: The clothes are a generation out of date and the paint has crazed like an old plate. These children would be grown by now, if anyone had let them grow.
-> END

~ portrait_history_fail
Narrator: Old, says the brushwork. How old, it won't say.
-> END

~ strahd_letter
narrate examine:strahd_letter
Narrator: A short letter in an elegant, unhurried hand, signed with a single ornate S.
Strahd: Gustav. You write again to offer me your devotion, as if it were a coin I had dropped. I did not ask for it, and I do not owe you for it.
Strahd: Your friends may keep their candles in the cellar. I find them touching, the way a dog is touching when it brings home something dead.
Strahd: I promise you nothing. Perhaps one evening I will come to dinner. Do not trouble to set a place for me. I will choose my own.
if not flag.death_house_read_strahd_letter
  set death_house_read_strahd_letter
  set death_house_lore += 1
endif
interject name:kip_smudgewick: Not one promise in it. Mum says read the small print. There isn't any. That's worse.
if not name:kip_smudgewick
  interject class:wizard: He wrote this as if he had all the time in the world. I suspect he does.
endif
-> END

~ deeds
narrate examine:durst_deeds
Narrator: Deeds and titles tied with string: this house, a vineyard plot long since sold, and a windmill on the Old Svalich Road, its sails sketched in the margin by a bored clerk.
set death_house_found_deeds
interject name:ratatoille: A windmill. A windmill means flour, and flour means bread. Not that I would... I am a wizard. Purely academic.
if not name:ratatoille
  interject background:criminal: Paper says it's ours if we want it. Paper says a lot of things.
endif
-> END

~ harpsichord
narrate examine:harpsichord
if flag.death_house_harpsichord_played
  if flag.death_house_knows_lullaby
    Narrator: The lullaby is still in your head. It will be for a while.
  else
    Narrator: The keys are silent, and they stay that way.
  endif
  -> END
endif
Narrator: Sheet music stands open on the rack: a lullaby, copied out in a careful, loving hand. Across the top someone has written a single letter, W.
* [Performance DC 11] Play it. (one try) -> play_ok | play_fail
* [Investigation DC 13] Study the notation until you can hear it without playing. (one try) -> read_ok | read_fail
* Leave it. -> END

~ play_ok
set death_house_harpsichord_played
set death_house_knows_lullaby
Narrator: The tune is simple and sad, the kind you sing over a crib at two in the morning. As the last note fades, a second pair of hands nobody can see plays it again, softly, from the beginning.
interject name:liriel_dawnsong: A cradle song, sung every night by someone who loved him. Keep it close. Someone in this house will know it.
if not name:liriel_dawnsong
  interject class:bard: I'll keep that one. A tune like that is a key to someone.
endif
-> END

~ play_fail
set death_house_harpsichord_played
Narrator: {name}'s fingers stumble. The keys go dead under them one by one, as if the instrument has decided you are not the right person to play this.
-> END

~ read_ok
set death_house_harpsichord_played
set death_house_knows_lullaby
Narrator: The notes resolve in {name}'s head into a lullaby, slow and simple, and very tired. Somewhere upstairs, very faintly, someone else is humming it too.
interject name:wren_featherfoot: Got it. Three-four, rocks like a cradle. I've busked worse for coppers, and I'll be humming this for a week.
-> END

~ read_fail
set death_house_harpsichord_played
Narrator: Ink, staves, a smudge that might be a tear. The notes refuse to become a tune, and the stand tips the music face down as if embarrassed for you.
-> END

~ nursemaid_letters
narrate examine:nursemaid_letters
Narrator: Letters from Gustav Durst to the household's nursemaid, tied in blue ribbon. They begin politely and stop being polite.
Narrator: The last says the mistress knows, the child must be kept quiet, and both of them will be cared for. Under it, in her hand: Walter, and a small drawn sun.
if not flag.death_house_learned_walter
  set death_house_learned_walter
  set death_house_lore += 1
endif
interject name:thistle: "Cared for." That's a lie. I can smell a lie on paper.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Walter was the nurse's baby. Hers and the master's. That's what the lady knew. Isn't it.
if not name:thistle
  interject class:cleric: "Cared for." I've read that phrase on too many graves.
endif
-> END

~ crib
narrate examine:nursery_crib
Narrator: The crib is freshly made, the blanket folded neatly around nothing at all.
if flag.death_house_learned_walter
  Narrator: Walter slept here, for a little while.
endif
interject class:cleric: Whoever kept this crib made never stopped hoping. That's the saddest kind of housekeeping.
-> END
